"""Single-point mutants of experiments/exp_peakcount.py (registries, split rule, front end, classical counters, path selection rules, scoring, bootstrap, verdict, report, stages), run with

    venv/bin/python tools/mutants.py --exp exp_peakcount --tests tests/test_exp_peakcount.py --raw results/ml/peakcount_raw.npz --md results/ml/peakcount.md \\
        --out-env PC_OUT --spec tools/mutants/peakcount_own.py --work <scratch>/mut --slot a --extra results/ml/peakcount_freeze.json

(id, what, old, new, k): `old` replaced by `new` at its k-th occurrence. P00 is the control (no change). The kill criterion is the tests file alone.

Result (118 entries: the control and 117 mutants). First pass with the first test file: 61 killed, 56 survived (mostly registry constants, the front end, and edge cases of the counters).
Tests were then added for every survivor (pinned registries and front-end values, edge cases, dispatch, scoring, bootstrap, verdict boundaries, report rows, the stages with stubs) and the 56 earlier survivors re-run:
52 killed; three more (C13 dispatch of the relative counter, Q15 dispatch of the paths, F13 window of the position offset) needed a sharper test and were re-run once more: all killed. Final: 116 of 117 mutants killed by tests/test_exp_peakcount.py (44 tests, about 45 s); the control passes.
The 61 mutants killed in the first pass were NOT re-run against the final test file (about 1 h of CPU); the final file is a superset of the assertions of the first, except that two weak tests (tune, position shift) were replaced by stronger ones.
One equivalent survivor, documented:
  C07  `g / g.sum()` -> `g` in the matched filter: the filter output is divided by its own 95th percentile right afterwards, so the scale of the template cancels exactly (up to floating-point rounding).
"""
